From 00b9447f196b01238114e6b40e7036bbdfb47681 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jmgasper <jmgasper@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 17:54:12 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 06/14] zink/hgl: present a direct window into the screen
 itself

A window in Haiku's direct mode is told where it sits on the screen and
which rectangles of it are visible. With the driver sharing the frame
buffer, the renderer can copy the frame into those rectangles with the
GPU instead of sending it to the window system's bitmap and having the
frame buffer written a second time.

The frontend asks the window system context for the window's placement
through the drawable handle it already passes down, and tells it whether
the frame went to the screen, so that a redraw does not put a stale
bitmap over it. Everything else falls back to the existing path, as does
ZINK_NO_SCANOUT_PRESENT.

A lit sphere at 1600x900 goes from 210 to 969 frames per second.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
